On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:56:21 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Tell me more about plugins. Specifically, how can you implement plugins
> in a monorepo?

As before, [F] marks something I read in the docs or ran here, and [I]
marks my inference. I ran the [T] checks with Claude Code 2.1.296.

- [F] A plugin is a directory holding .claude-plugin/plugin.json and a
  skills/<name>/SKILL.md for each skill. It can also carry agents,
  hooks, and MCP servers. Its skills are namespaced as
  <plugin>:<skill> [a].

- [F] A monorepo can hold plugins in two ways. The first is plain
  directories passed with `claude --plugin-dir <dir>`. The flag can be
  repeated, the plugin loads in place for that session only, and
  nothing is installed [b][c].

- [F] The second is to make the repo a marketplace. You add
  .claude-plugin/marketplace.json at the root, and it lists each plugin
  by a relative path such as "./claude/build/by-commit". Then
  .claude/settings.json turns plugins on with enabledPlugins
  {"<name>@<marketplace>": true}. Plugins with a relative path load in
  place from the checkout, so edits take effect on the next session
  [d][c].

- [I] For this case a marketplace adds machinery and buys nothing. It
  exists for distributing plugins to other people and machines. We
  only need to choose one plugin per launch, which --plugin-dir does
  by itself.

> I could make those as claude skills. Now when I run it, I don't just
> want to execute a specific one - I want to ensure claude doesn't know
> about or have access to the other one.

Here is how each control behaves, tested by asking a fresh `claude -p`
to list the skills in its context:

- [T] `--plugin-dir ./build-by-commit` lists only
  build-by-commit:build. build-by-spec is not loaded, so Claude has no
  trace of it.

- [F] Every skill under .claude/skills loads in every session started
  in the repo [a]. That is visible by default, the opposite of what
  you want.

- [T] Even so, a skill under .claude/skills can be hidden for one
  launch:

      claude --settings '{"skillOverrides":{"build-by-spec":"off"}}'

  With that flag, build-by-spec dropped out of the list. In the docs,
  "off" means Claude does not see the skill and /name is hidden [e].
  The catch is that this is a deny list. Each new variant shows up in
  every session until someone adds it to the list.

- [T] A deny rule such as `--disallowedTools "Skill(build-by-spec)"`
  does not meet your bar. The skill stayed in the list, and the rule
  only refuses the call [f]. The same holds for the Skill(...) denies
  in our .claude/settings.json today.

- [F] With disable-model-invocation: true, the description stays out
  of context, but you can still run the skill as /name [a]. It hides
  the skill, but it does not remove it.

> Maybe in my case I don't
> actually put it in .claude/skills and instead just put it under src and
> launch claude with it?

Yes, I recommend that. Keep each implementation as its own plugin
outside .claude/, and have the launcher pick one with --plugin-dir. The
skill you don't pick is never loaded, so it is absent by construction.
Nothing has to remember to hide it. It also fits "Lab repo shouldn't
operate on itself" [1], since what is loaded is decided at launch and
does not depend on the tree's .claude/.

One layout makes the variants interchangeable:

    src/claude-build/by-commit/.claude-plugin/plugin.json
    src/claude-build/by-commit/skills/build/SKILL.md
    src/claude-build/by-spec/.claude-plugin/plugin.json
    src/claude-build/by-spec/skills/build/SKILL.md

- [I] If both manifests use the name "build", the skill is build:build
  whichever variant is loaded. prompt.md can then say "use the build
  skill" without knowing which variant is in play.

- [F] Loading two plugins with the same name together is not a clean
  error. Among installed and skills-dir copies, the --plugin-dir copy
  wins [c]. The docs do not say what happens with two --plugin-dir
  copies of the same name. The launcher should pass exactly one.

- [I] If you want both visible side by side, for example when comparing
  them in one session, give the manifests distinct names
  (build-by-commit, build-by-spec) instead.

- [F] lab-process-mail runs `claude --permission-mode auto -p ...` in
  the workspace today, with no plugin flags. Choosing a variant would
  take a --build <variant> option that adds
  --plugin-dir src/claude-build/<variant>. Its tests already stub
  claude, so they can check the flag is passed.

- [F] One caution: `--plugin-dir` also accepts a folder of plugins
  [g]. Pointed at src/claude-build/, it would load both. Pass the
  variant's own directory.

